The circuit consists of an ideal diode connected in parallel with the resistor \(R\). For an ideal diode, <ul><li>During the positive half-cycle of the source current, the diode is forward biased and behaves as a short circuit. Hence, the entire current flows through the diode and no current flows through the resistor.</li><li>During the negative half-cycle, the diode is reverse biased and behaves as an open circuit. Hence, the entire source current flows through the resistor.</li></ul> Here, \[i_R(t)=
\begin{cases}
0, &amp; 0\leq \omega t\leq \pi,\\ -\pi\sin(\omega t), &amp; \pi\leq \omega t\leq 2\pi.
\end{cases}\] Thus, the resistor current is a half-wave rectified sine wave. The average current over one complete cycle is \[I_{\text{avg}}
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
\int_{\pi}^{2\pi}
\left(-\pi\sin\theta\right)\,d\theta\] \[=
-\frac{1}{2}
\left[-\cos\theta\right]_{\pi}^{2\pi}=
\frac{1}{2}
\left[\cos(2\pi)-\cos(\pi)\right]\] \[=
\frac{1}{2}
\left[1-(-1)\right]
=
1~\text{mA}\] Hence, the magnitude of the average current through the resistor is \[\boxed{1.00}\]

The circuit is an inverting amplifier with <img src="img/ef4fbaffdc88abba.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> \[R_1=1\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
R_f=100\,\text{k}\Omega.\] Hence, the noise gain is \[A_N=1+\frac{R_f}{R_1}
=1+\frac{100}{1}
=101.\] The equivalent resistance seen at the inverting input is \[R_{eq}=R_1\parallel R_f
=\frac{1\times100}{1+100}\,\text{k}\Omega
\approx0.99\,\text{k}\Omega.\] The output error due to the input offset voltage is \[V_{o,OS}
=A_NV_{OS}
=101\times10\,\mu\text{V}
=1010\,\mu\text{V}.\] The output error due to the input bias current is \[V_{o,IB}
=I_BR_f
=(1\,\text{nA})(100\,\text{k}\Omega)
=100\,\mu\text{V}.\] For the worst case, both errors add in the same direction. \[V_{o,\max}
=1010+100
=1110\,\mu\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{1110}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Output offset due to input offset voltage is \(A_NV_{OS}\).</li><li>Input bias current flowing through the feedback resistor produces an output error of \(I_BR_f\).</li></ul></div>

Since the op-amps are ideal, \[V_{-1}=V_{+1}=V_{S1},\qquad
V_{-2}=V_{+2}=V_{S2}.\] The three resistors between \(V_{o1}\) and \(V_{o2}\) are equal. Hence, \[V_{S1}=\frac{2V_{o1}+V_{o2}}{3},\] \[V_{S2}=\frac{V_{o1}+2V_{o2}}{3}.\] Solving, \[V_{o1}=2V_{S1}-V_{S2},\] \[V_{o2}=2V_{S2}-V_{S1}.\] <img src="img/ec889da2aa6b68f7.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> The output \(V_o\) is taken from the midpoint of two equal resistors connected between \(V_{o1}\) and \(V_{o2}\). Therefore, \[V_o=\frac{V_{o1}+V_{o2}}{2}
=\frac{V_{S1}+V_{S2}}{2}.\] Substituting \[V_{S1}=3+0.1\sin(300t),\] \[V_{S2}=-2+0.11\sin(300t),\] gives \[V_o
=\frac{1+0.21\sin(300t)}{2}
=0.5+0.105\sin(300t).\] Hence, the average value of \(V_o\) is \[\boxed{0.50}\]

Initially, the \(10\,\text{nF}\) feedback capacitor stores \[Q_0=1\,\text{nC}.\] Hence, the initial output voltage is \[V_o(0)=\frac{Q_0}{C_f}
=\frac{1\,\text{nC}}{10\,\text{nF}}
=100\,\text{mV}.\] When \(V_s\) is High, the \(1\,\text{nF}\) capacitor is connected to \[V_{in}=100\,\text{mV},\] so it stores \[Q_s=C_sV_{in}
=(1\,\text{nF})(100\,\text{mV})
=0.1\,\text{nC}.\] When \(V_s\) is Low, this charge is transferred to the feedback capacitor. Therefore, the output changes by \[\Delta V_o
=-\frac{Q_s}{C_f}
=-\frac{0.1\,\text{nC}}{10\,\text{nF}}
=-10\,\text{mV}\] per switching cycle. The switching frequency is \(1\,\text{kHz}\). Thus, in \[t=20\,\text{ms},\] the number of cycles is \[N=1000\times0.02=20.\] Hence, \[V_o(20\,\text{ms})
=100-20(10)
=-100\,\text{mV}.\] Therefore, \[|V_o|=100\,\text{mV}.\] \[\boxed{100}\]

We are given a bar chart representing the discrete probability mass function \(P(x)\) of a random variable \(x\). We need to analyze the relative values of its mean, median, and mode. <img src="img/fccd8682fc993852.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> We are given a bar chart representing a probability mass function \(P(x)\) for a discrete random variable \(x\). <strong>Step 1: Analyze the symmetry of the distribution</strong> <ul><li>Observing the plot, \(P(x)\) is perfectly symmetric about the vertical axis \(x = 0\) (i.e., \(P(-x) = P(x)\) for all \(x\)).</li><li>For any distribution that is symmetric about a point \(c\), the **mean** and **median** both equal \(c\).</li><li>Here, the center of symmetry is \(x = 0\), so: \[	ext{mean} = 0, \quad 	ext{median} = 0 \implies 	ext{mean} = 	ext{median}\]</li></ul> <strong>Step 2: Identify the mode(s) of the distribution</strong><br/><ul><li>The **mode** is the value of \(x\) at which the probability mass function \(P(x)\) reaches its maximum peak height.</li><li>Inspecting the histogram, the tallest bars occur at \(x = -13\) and \(x = 13\).</li><li>Thus, this is a bimodal distribution with modes at: \[	ext{mode} = -13 	ext{ and } 13\]</li><li>Since \(	ext{mode} 
e 0\), we have: \[	ext{mean} = 	ext{median} 
e 	ext{mode}\]</li></ul> This corresponds to choice A. \[ oxed{	ext{A. mean = median } 
e 	ext{ mode}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For any distribution that is perfectly symmetric, the mean and the median are always exactly equal and sit at the center of symmetry. If the distribution is bimodal with its peaks located away from the center, the mode value will be different from the mean and median.</li></ul></div>
